      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "In the past years coronary atherosclerosis and plaque rupture have been characterized not only as a problem of growth of smooth vascular cells, of activated thrombocytes and coagulation but also of inflammation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "PARAMETERS OF INFLAMMATION",
          "content": "Several studies have demonstrated the importance of C-reactive protein as a marker for cardiovascular complications. Also interleukin-6, tumor necrosis factor-alpha and adhesion molecules are discussed as predictors of a poor prognosis in patients with acute coronary syndromes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "RISK STRATIFICATION",
          "content": "A positive troponin T test is correlated with an increased rate of early complications; thus, these patients benefit from an aggressive therapy with GP IIb/IIIa receptor antagonists and acute coronary intervention. The C-reactive protein is predictive for late complications such as restenosis, reinfarction and death due to cardiac events; these patients benefit from close controls and from an anti-inflammatory therapy with acetylsalicyclic acid and statins."
        }
      ]},
